Uses memmove for the two copies of gas whose source and destination can overlap.

md_convert_frag (gas/config/tc-mips.c) replaces the first variant of a relaxed
macro with the second one, which follows it in the same frag:
memcpy (fixptr - old, fixptr, new). When the second variant is the longer one,
the ranges overlap: the NO_PIC load/store from sym(reg) (addu + <op> with
GPREL, 8 bytes, replaced by lui + addu + <op>, 12 bytes), and a few more. This
as is an i386 binary that takes memcpy from the libc it runs on, and the
implementation is chosen at run time: glibc's i386 __memcpy_ssse3 copies a block
shorter than 48 bytes backwards when the low byte of the source address is below
that of the destination. The last word of the new variant then overwrites the
lui before it is copied, and the object gets <op> $r,0($r) carrying the
R_MIPS_HI16 meant for the lui. Which macros are hit depends on where the frag
lands in the heap: reading from stdin or from a file, or a different -o name,
changes the set. A Linux 2.4.17 kernel assembled this way oopses.

input_scrub_next_buffer (gas/input-scrub.c) moves the partial line left at the
end of a 32 KB read to the start of the buffer, also with memcpy; the ranges
overlap when that line is longer than what precedes it in the read (a line of
some 16 KB or more). The i386 memcpy of glibc 2.44 copies such a block
forwards, which is what the code needs, but nothing guarantees it: with a memcpy
that copies backwards, gas crashes on such a line.

Where the ranges do not overlap, or memcpy happens to copy forwards, memmove
gives the same bytes. Only gas changes: ld and the other binutils do not link
these files.

--- a/gas/config/tc-mips.c
+++ b/gas/config/tc-mips.c
@@ -12024,7 +12024,7 @@
       fixptr = fragp->fr_literal + fragp->fr_fix;
 
       if (new > 0)
-	memcpy (fixptr - old, fixptr, new);
+	memmove (fixptr - old, fixptr, new);
 
       fragp->fr_fix += new - old;
     }
--- a/gas/input-scrub.c
+++ b/gas/input-scrub.c
@@ -316,8 +316,8 @@
 
   if (partial_size)
     {
-      memcpy (buffer_start + BEFORE_SIZE, partial_where,
-	      (unsigned int) partial_size);
+      memmove (buffer_start + BEFORE_SIZE, partial_where,
+	       (unsigned int) partial_size);
       memcpy (buffer_start + BEFORE_SIZE, save_source, AFTER_SIZE);
     }
   limit = input_file_give_next_buffer (buffer_start
